  "body": {
    "id": "lem:positive-width",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every compatible strictly positive input, \\(qU-qL=cU-cL+w11\\ge w11>0\\); thus the two experiments never point-identify \\(q\\) in the stated positive-table model.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "qU",
      "qL",
      "cU",
      "cL",
      "w11",
      "q"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:sharp-risk-interval"
    ],
    "justification": "The width identity distinguishes compatibility from identification and gives a required sanity reduction.",
    "gap": "The generic methods return numerical intervals but do not state the strictly positive nonidentification margin for this family.",
    "consumer": "An experimental designer learns that these two regimes alone cannot point-identify the joint-intervention risk.",
    "proof_tex": "The endpoint identities proved in \\(\\text{thm:sharp-risk-interval}\\) give\n\\[\nqL=a+b-cU,\n\\qquad\nqU=a+b-cL+w11.\n\\]\nSubtracting yields\n\\[\nqU-qL=cU-cL+w11.\n\\]\nCompatibility gives \\(cU-cL\\ge0\\).  Strict positivity of both tables gives \\(0<p,r<1\\), and hence\n\\[\nw11=(1-p)(1-r)>0.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(qU-qL\\ge w11>0\\).  Since the sharp identified set is \\([qL,qU]\\), it contains more than one value of \\(q\\); thus the two supplied experiments do not point-identify the joint-intervention risk anywhere in the stated strictly positive compatible model.",
